\chapter{Formal semantics}
\label{ch:semantics}

We introduce a calculus grounded in a separation between values and computational obligations. This chapter establishes the formal semantic model underlying that separation, beginning with the domains in which computations produce values or witnesses and proceeding toward the obligations that arise from those witnesses. The model distinguishes the value produced by a computation from the computational consequences associated with its evaluation, thereby preserving the strong value space established in the introduction.

We first define the result domain and its partition into pure values and witnesses. We then characterize the obligations produced by computations and their occurrence across branches, establishing the structure required for their linear treatment. Finally, we formalize the morphisms through which witnesses are reified into obligations and obligations are either consumed to restore values or deferred by producing subsequent obligations. These constructions together define the computational model in which failure is not a value, nor an implicit control-flow escape, but an explicitly witnessed obligation whose consumption is statically accounted for.

\section{Function evaluation and the result domain}
\label{sect:func-eval}

Given a function $f$, let $A$ denote its input-value domain, $B$ the universe of branches, and $B_f\subseteq B$ the set of branches occurring in the body of $f$. Evaluation proceeds in two stages: an input first determines the branch to be executed, and that branch is then evaluated:

$$
A \xrightarrow{\text{select}_f}
B_f \xrightarrow{\text{eval}}
R.
$$

The branch-selection step captures the control-flow structure of the function. For a given input $a\in A$, $\text{select}_f$ identifies the branch $b\in B_f$ whose execution is determined by that input. The subsequent evaluation of $b$ produces an element of the result domain $R$.

The result domain is partitioned into values and witnesses:

$$
\boxed{
  R=V\uplus W.
}
$$

Here, $V$ is the domain of ordinary language values, while $W$ is the domain of witnesses produced during evaluation. The disjointness is intentional: a witness is not itself a language value and therefore cannot be used interchangeably with an element of $V$.

In particular, ordinary evaluation has the form

$$
b\in B_f
\quad\overset{\text{eval}}{\longmapsto}\quad
v\in V,
$$

whereas evaluation may also produce a witness,

$$
b\in B_f
\quad\overset{\text{eval}}{\longmapsto}\quad
w\in W.
$$

Thus, $R$ represents the complete range of outcomes of branch evaluation, while $V$ represents only those outcomes that are ordinary values.

The value domain is itself distinguished from the bottom element:

$$
V\not\to\bot.
$$

That is, $bot$ does not belong to the ordinary value domain and is not represented as a value produced by successful evaluation. The distinction is important because the semantics does not model the effects represented by witnesses as ordinary bottom-valued computation. Instead, such effects are represented explicitly by elements of $W$.

The set $B_f$ is a function-specific subset of the general branch domain:

$$
B_f\hookrightarrow B.
$$

This distinction allows the semantics to reason about the branches of a particular function without identifying them with the entire syntactic universe of possible branches. In particular, the structure of $B_f$ determines which branch-specific phenomena can arise during the evaluation of $f$. The resulting structure is summarized in Figure~\ref{fig:semantic-structure}.

\begin{figure}[ht]
  \centering
  \input{figures/semantic-structure.latex}
  \caption{Semantic structure of function evaluation. A function selects a branch from $B_f$ and evaluates it to either a value $v\in V$ or a witness $w\in W$. Values and witnesses form the disjoint result domain $R=V\uplus W$. The lower portions of the diagram describe the internal structure of values and witnesses, which is developed in the following sections. Squiggly arrows denote derived relations rather than ordinary semantic morphisms.}
  \label{fig:semantic-structure}
\end{figure}

At this point, the semantics deliberately leaves the internal structure of $W$ unspecified. In particular, not every witness necessarily carries a linear obligation. The witness domain and its relationship with obligation occurrences are introduced next.

\section{Witnesses and obligation occurrences}
\label{sec:witness-obligation}

The witness domain is itself partitioned according to whether a witness exposes a linear obligation:

$$
\boxed{
  W=W_0\uplus W_L.
}
$$

Here, $W_0$ is the set of witnesses that expose no linear obligation, while $W_L$ is the set of witnesses that expose a linear obligation. This distinction allows the semantics to represent computational phenomena that do not directly correspond to ordinary values without conflating them with linear obligations themselves.

Witnesses in $W_L$ expose their associated obligation occurrences through the operation

$$
\operatorname{expose}:W_L\to L_f\hookrightarrow L.
$$

The codomain $L_f$ is specific to the function $f$: it contains the linear obligation occurrences that arise from the branches of $f$. The larger set $L$ denotes the universe of linear obligations.

For each branch $b\in B_f$, let

$$
\operatorname{obligations}(b)
$$

denote the collection of obligation occurrences associated with that branch. The obligations occurring throughout the function are then defined by the disjoint union

$$\boxed{
  L_f=
    \biguplus_{b\in B_f}
    \operatorname{obligations}(b).
}
$$

The use of the disjoint union is significant. The semantics tracks occurrences of obligations, rather than merely the distinct obligation names that occur in the function. Consequently, two different branches may contain occurrences corresponding to the same underlying obligation while those occurrences remain distinct.

For example, if $b_1,b_2\in B_f$ are distinct branches and both contain an occurrence corresponding to $l\in L$, then the two occurrences are nevertheless distinct elements of $L_f$. Their distinction is inherited from the branches in which they occur. Thus, branch structure is preserved when the obligations of a function are collected.

This also explains why $L_f$ is associated with the function rather than being identified directly with $L$. The set $L$ describes the universe of possible obligations, whereas $L_f$ records the particular obligation occurrences induced by the branches of $f$.

The resulting relationship can therefore be summarized as

$$
W_L \xrightarrow{\operatorname{expose}}
L_f \hookrightarrow L,
$$

where a witness identifies an obligation occurrence belonging to the function, and that occurrence belongs to the larger universe of linear obligations.

\section{Tacet and obligation reification}
\label{sec:tacet-reif}

Linear obligations are not themselves values produced directly by the language. Instead, the language provides the `tacet` construct as a syntactic mechanism through which a computation can produce a witness that subsequently exposes an obligation.

Tags form a distinguished class of values,

$$
V_T\hookrightarrow V.
$$

Not every tag participates in this mechanism. Let $V_{\widetilde T}\subseteq V_T$ denote the subset of tags that admit interpretation by `tacet`:

$$
V_{\widetilde T}\hookrightarrow V_T.
$$

The `tacet` construct is part of the language syntax. It does not itself produce a value or a linear obligation. During evaluation, a `tacet` expression whose tag belongs to $V_{\widetilde T}$ is interpreted as a linear witness:

$$
\boxed{
  \operatorname{tacet}:V_{\widetilde T}\rightsquigarrow W_L.
}
$$

The squiggly arrow is intentional: `tacet` is not an ordinary semantic morphism from values to witnesses. Rather, it represents the interpretation of a syntactic construct during evaluation.

The resulting witness then exposes an obligation occurrence associated with the function:

$$
V_{\widetilde T} \overset{\operatorname{tacet}}{\rightsquigarrow}
W_L \xrightarrow{\operatorname{expose}}
L_f \hookrightarrow L.
$$

Thus, the obligation is introduced into the semantic computation indirectly. The language produces a tag-valued syntactic construct; evaluation interprets that construct as a witness; and the witness exposes the corresponding obligation occurrence.

This distinction is important because it preserves the separation between the language's value domain and its linear-obligation domain. In particular,

$$
V_{\widetilde T}\subseteq V
$$

does not imply that $L$ is introduced into $V$. The tag remains a value, while its interpretation produces an element of $W_L$, and only the witness exposes the associated element of $L_f$.

For a function $f$, the complete path from a tacet-able tag to an obligation is therefore

$$
\boxed{
  V_{\widetilde T} \rightsquigarrow
  W_L \longrightarrow
  L_f \hookrightarrow
  L.
}
$$

This gives the semantics a three-level separation between values, witnesses, and linear obligations: values inhabit $V$, witnesses inhabit $W$, and obligations inhabit $L$. The `tacet` construct provides the syntactic bridge between the first two, while `expose` provides the semantic bridge between the latter two.

\section{Discharge morphisms}
\label{sec:discharge-mor}

Linear obligations introduced into $L_f$ cannot leak or be silently discarded. They must be resolved via handling contexts ($\mathcal{H}$) through three distinct discharge operations.

$$
\begin{array}{rcccl}
	\text{Homomorphic Reifier:} &
		\operatorname{reify}_T &
			: &
			W_L(T) \times \mathcal{H}_T \longrightarrow V(T) \\
	\text{Polymorphic Reifier:} &
		\operatorname{reify}_{\forall} &
			: &
				W_L(T) \times \mathcal{H}_U \longrightarrow V(U) \\
	\text{Obligation Deferral:} &
		\operatorname{defer} &
			: &
				L_f \times \mathcal{H}_{\text{defer}} \longrightarrow L_f' \end{array}
$$

A \emph{Type-Preserving Reifier ($\operatorname{reify}_T$)} consumes a linear witness $w \in W_L(T)$ and returns an ordinary value $v \in V(T)$ of the same type. A \emph{Type-Transforming Reifier ($\operatorname{reify}_{\forall}$)} consumes a linear witness $w \in W_L(T)$ and projects execution into an ordinary value $v \in V(U)$ of a different target type $U$. Finally, an \emph{Obligation Deferral ($\operatorname{defer}$)} consumes an active obligation occurrence $l_f \in L_f$ while simultaneously instantiating a new successor obligation $l'_f \in L_f'$.

Obligation propagation forms an inductive chain along an execution branch $b \in B_f$:

$$
l_f^{(0)} \;\xrightarrow{\operatorname{defer}}\; l_f^{(1)} \;\xrightarrow{\operatorname{defer}}\; \dots \;\xrightarrow{\operatorname{defer}}\; l_f^{(k)} \;\xrightarrow{\quad\operatorname{reify}_{T \mid \forall}\quad}\; v \in V
$$

\emph{Local Linearity}

Each $\operatorname{defer}$ step consumes $l_f^{(i)}$ strictly once.

\emph{Global Branch Totality}

A branch $b \in B_f$ cannot complete evaluation into $V$ while an unconsumed obligation remains in $L_f$. Every chain must end in a reification step ($\operatorname{reify}_T$ or $\operatorname{reify}_{\forall}$).

\section{Discharge typing rules}
\label{sec:discharge-rules}

The formal typing rules for obligation discharge are expressed as intuitionistic linear typing judgments:

$$\Gamma; \Delta \vdash e : \tau$$

- $\Gamma$ (Lexical Context): Unrestricted environment mapping immutable identifiers to ordinary value types.
- $\Delta$ (Linear Context): Multiset tracking active, unconsumed obligation occurrences ($\Delta = \{l_1 : T_1, l_2 : T_2, \dots\}$).
- Multiplicative Context Splitting ($\Delta = \Delta_1, \Delta_2$): Disjoint distribution of active obligations across subterms.

\subsection{Homomorphic Reifier ($\text{T-Reify}_T$)}

Consumes a linear witness of payload type $T$ and evaluates a handler that returns a value of the exact same type $T \in V$.

$$\frac{\Gamma; \Delta_1 \vdash e_w : \operatorname{Witness}(T) \quad \Gamma, x : T; \Delta_2 \vdash e_h : T}{\Gamma; \Delta_1, \Delta_2 \vdash \operatorname{reify}_T(e_w, x \mapsto e_h) : T}$$

Premises:
1. Expression $e_w$ produces a linear witness carrying obligation $l \in \Delta_1$.
2. Handling expression $e_h$ consumes payload variable $x : T$ and evaluates to an ordinary value in $V(T)$.

Linear Context Effect: Obligation $l$ is discharged. No obligation residue remains from $e_w$.

\subsection{Polymorphic Reifier ($\text{T-Reify}_{\forall}$)}

Consumes a linear witness of payload type $T$ and projects execution into an arbitrary ordinary target value type $U \in V$.

$$\frac{\Gamma; \Delta_1 \vdash e_w : \operatorname{Witness}(T) \quad \Gamma, x : T; \Delta_2 \vdash e_h : U}{\Gamma; \Delta_1, \Delta_2 \vdash \operatorname{reify}_{\forall}(e_w, x \mapsto e_h) : U}$$

Premises:
1. Expression $e_w$ produces a linear witness carrying obligation $l \in \Delta_1$.
2. Handling expression $e_h$ consumes payload variable $x : T$ and produces a value of target type $U$.

Linear Context Effect: Obligation $l$ is fully consumed, transitioning computation directly into value domain $V(U)$.

\subsection{Transitive Obligation Deferral ($\text{T-Defer}$)}

Consumes an existing obligation occurrence $l \in \Delta_1$ while instantiating a fresh successor obligation occurrence $l' \in \Delta_2$.

$$\frac{\Gamma; \Delta_1 \vdash e_w : \operatorname{Witness}(T) \quad \Gamma, x : T; \Delta_2 \vdash e_d : \operatorname{Witness}(U)}{\Gamma; \Delta_1, \Delta_2 \vdash \operatorname{defer}(e_w, x \mapsto e_d) : \operatorname{Witness}(U)}$$

Premises:
1. Expression $e_w$ introduces obligation occurrence $l : \operatorname{Witness}(T)$ in context $\Delta_1$.
2. Deferral handler $e_d$ consumes payload $x : T$ and emits a new witness carrying obligation occurrence $l' : \operatorname{Witness}(U)$ in context $\Delta_2$.

Linear Context Effect: Obligation $l$ is consumed locally, while successor obligation $l'$ is threaded downstream in the linear context.

\subsection{Summary of Linear Context Transformations}

$$\begin{array}{rcccl} \text{T-Reify}_T & : & \Delta, l : \operatorname{Witness}(T) & \longrightarrow & \Delta \\ \text{T-Reify}_{\forall} & : & \Delta, l : \operatorname{Witness}(T) & \longrightarrow & \Delta \\ \text{T-Defer} & : & \Delta, l : \operatorname{Witness}(T) & \longrightarrow & \Delta, l' : \operatorname{Witness}(U) \end{array}$$

Together, these judgments establish that no linear obligation $l \in L_f$ can be silently dropped—it must either be discharged into value space $V$ or transitively transformed into a successor obligation $l'$.
